.TH MATHERR 3
.SH NAME
matherr \- error-handling function for the math library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <math.h>

int
matherr(x)
struct exception *x;
.fi
.PP
cc ... \-lm
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a function of the math library meets an error, it calls
.B matherr
with a pointer to a structure that describes it:
.PP
.nf
	struct exception {
		int type;	/* the kind of error */
		char *name;	/* the function, as "log" */
		double arg1;	/* its arguments */
		double arg2;
		double retval;	/* the value it will return */
	};
.fi
.PP
.I type
is one of these, defined in <math.h>:
.TP 12
.B DOMAIN
an argument outside the function's domain, as sqrt(\-1.0)
.TP
.B SING
a singularity, as log(0.0)
.TP
.B OVERFLOW
a result too large to be held
.TP
.B UNDERFLOW
a result too small to be told from zero
.TP
.B TLOSS
a total loss of significance, as sin(1e20)
.PP
The library's own matherr returns 0. The function then sets errno, to
EDOM for DOMAIN and SING errors and to ERANGE for the others; for
DOMAIN, SING and TLOSS errors it writes a message, such as "log: SING
error", on the standard error; and it returns
.IR retval .
.PP
A program may define a matherr of its own, which the link editor then
uses instead. If it returns nonzero, the function sets no errno and
writes no message. In either case the function returns
.IR retval ,
which matherr may have changed.
.SH EXAMPLES
To make log(0.0) return 0 quietly, and leave the other errors alone:
.PP
.nf
	#include <math.h>

	int
	matherr(x)
	struct exception *x;
	{
		if (x\->type == SING && strcmp(x\->name, "log") == 0) {
			x\->retval = 0.0;
			return 1;
		}
		return 0;
	}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3), trig(3), sinh(3), hypot(3), intro(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B matherr
function is that of System V.
